From: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 08:20:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] DwUsbHostDxe: fail fast when the DWC2 core is absent

DwHcReset() arms one deadline and hands it to DwCoreInit() and
DwHcInit(), whose every register handshake goes through Wait4Bit() -- a
tight MmioRead32/CheckEvent busy-poll with no delay in it. When a polled
bit never changes the driver burns the entire budget on that one wait,
which on the Pi 5 was ten seconds of every boot before any USB device
was contacted at all.

Check whether there is a core there before starting the polled sequence:
GSNPSID reads back the Synopsys ID, whose top 16 bits are ASCII "OT" on
these OTG cores. Linux gates on the same register in
dwc2_check_core_version(), and so does u-boot's dwc2. A block that is
powered down reads all-zeroes or all-ones and is rejected in one MMIO
read, so an absent controller costs microseconds instead of seconds.

Two supporting changes:

  * DriverStart now honours DwHcReset()'s status. It was discarded, so a
    failed bring-up still published EFI_USB2_HC_PROTOCOL and handed
    UsbBusDxe a controller on which every transfer would time out.

  * DW_HC_RESET_TIMEOUT_MS 10s -> 1s. These handshakes complete in
    microseconds; the timeout only bounds the broken case, and one
    second is still ~1000x the hardware requirement.

Upstream-Status: Submittable [with an RPi5 platform port]
Signed-off-by: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
@@ -13,9 +13,23 @@
 #include "DwcHw.h"
 
 /*
- * Excessive (10s) timeout for reset.
+ * Bound for the whole reset/init sequence (DwCoreInit + DwHcInit share
+ * one deadline). The Synopsys core completes these register handshakes
+ * in microseconds, so this only ever matters when something is wrong --
+ * and Wait4Bit() busy-polls, so whatever is left of the budget is burnt
+ * as pure boot latency. One second is still ~1000x the hardware
+ * requirement; it used to be ten, which is a very long time to spend
+ * discovering that a controller is not there.
  */
-#define DW_HC_RESET_TIMEOUT_MS (10000)
+#define DW_HC_RESET_TIMEOUT_MS (1000)
+
+/*
+ * GSNPSID reads back the Synopsys core ID: the top 16 bits are ASCII
+ * "OT" on the OTG cores this driver drives. Linux's dwc2 gates on the
+ * same value (dwc2_check_core_version) and refuses the device otherwise.
+ */
+#define DWC2_GSNPSID_ID_MASK   0xFFFF0000
+#define DWC2_GSNPSID_OTG_ID    0x4F540000
 
   /*
    * TimerPeriodic to account for timeout processing
@@ -1402,6 +1416,59 @@
   return EFI_SUCCESS;
 }
 
+/**
+  Report whether a DWC2 core is actually present and responding at the
+  configured base address.
+
+  Worth one MMIO read before anything else, because nothing further down
+  fails fast: DwCoreReset() and DwHcInit() hand every register handshake
+  to Wait4Bit(), which busy-polls a shared deadline. A core that is
+  powered down reads all-zeroes or all-ones, no polled bit ever changes,
+  and the driver spends the entire DW_HC_RESET_TIMEOUT_MS budget before
+  giving up -- straight-line boot latency, with a dead port at the end
+  of it either way.
+
+  That is the Pi 5 case, not a hypothetical: the BCM2712 DWC2 behind the
+  USB-C connector sits in the USB power domain, and its firmware DTB node
+  is "status = disabled" with power-domains = <&power RPI_POWER_DOMAIN_USB>.
+  The VideoCore SET_POWER_STATE (USB HCD) mailbox call this driver makes
+  is the BCM283x interface and does not turn that domain on, so unless
+  something else has powered the block, there is nothing here to talk to.
+
+  @param[in] DwHc  The controller instance, with DwUsbBase set.
+
+  @retval TRUE   A DWC2 OTG core answered with a valid Synopsys ID.
+  @retval FALSE  Nothing is responding at DwUsbBase.
+**/
+STATIC
+BOOLEAN
+DwCorePresent (
+  IN  DWUSB_OTGHC_DEV *DwHc
+  )
+{
+  UINT32 Id;
+
+  Id = MmioRead32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + GSNPSID);
+  if ((Id & DWC2_GSNPSID_ID_MASK) != DWC2_GSNPSID_OTG_ID) {
+    DEBUG ((
+      DEBUG_ERROR,
+      "DwUsbHostDxe: no DWC2 core at 0x%lx (GSNPSID reads 0x%08x): the "
+      "block is unpowered, held in reset, or the base address is wrong\n",
+      (UINT64)DwHc->DwUsbBase,
+      Id
+      ));
+    return FALSE;
+  }
+
+  DEBUG ((
+    DEBUG_INFO,
+    "DwUsbHostDxe: DWC2 core at 0x%lx, GSNPSID 0x%08x\n",
+    (UINT64)DwHc->DwUsbBase,
+    Id
+    ));
+  return TRUE;
+}
+
 EFI_STATUS
 DwCoreInit (
   IN  DWUSB_OTGHC_DEV *DwHc,
@@ -1412,6 +1479,13 @@
   UINT32 UsbCfg = 0;
   EFI_STATUS Status;
 
+  //
+  // Bail before the first polled handshake, not after the deadline.
+  //
+  if (!DwCorePresent (DwHc)) {
+    return EFI_DEVICE_ERROR;
+  }
+
   UsbCfg = MmioRead32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + GUSBCFG);
 
   UsbCfg |= DWC2_GUSBCFG_ULPI_EXT_VBUS_DRV;
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DriverBinding.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DriverBinding.c
@@ -145,8 +145,18 @@
   /*
    * UsbBusDxe as of b4e96b82b4e2e47e95014b51787ba5b43abac784 expects
    * the HCD to do this. There is no agent invoking DwHcReset anymore.
+   *
+   * Its result is load-bearing: it is where the controller is actually
+   * brought up, so if it fails there is no host here. Publishing
+   * EFI_USB2_HC_PROTOCOL anyway just hands UsbBusDxe a controller whose
+   * every transfer will time out.
    */
-  DwHcReset (&DwHc->DwUsbOtgHc, EFI_USB_HC_RESET_HOST_CONTROLLER);
+  Status = DwHcReset (&DwHc->DwUsbOtgHc, EFI_USB_HC_RESET_HOST_CONTROLLER);
+  if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
+    DEBUG ((DEBUG_ERROR, "DwUsbHostDxe: controller reset failed: %r\n", Status));
+    goto Exit;
+  }
+
   DwHcSetState (&DwHc->DwUsbOtgHc, EfiUsbHcStateOperational);
 
   Status = gBS->InstallMultipleProtocolInterfaces (
